---
// Safe to edit.
/* ============================================================================
   ScrollProgress: the reading bar on long pages (overhaul package 2.4a)
   ============================================================================
   A 3px accent bar fixed to the top of the viewport that fills left to right
   as the visitor scrolls. Used on the case-study and journal-entry templates
   (plan ruling 6). It replaced ReadingProgress.astro, which needed a scroll
   listener: this one is pure CSS, a scroll-driven animation
   (animation-timeline: scroll()), so it ships no JavaScript at all.

   Where it shows:
     - only where the browser supports scroll-driven animations (@supports),
     - and only under prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference.
   Everywhere else it stays hidden (display: none), which is the honest
   fallback: no bar at all rather than a bar that never moves.

   Decorative, so aria-hidden. Render it AFTER the page's <article>: it is
   position: fixed, so its DOM place does not show, but as <main>'s first
   child it would make the first-screen reveal exemption miss the page header
   (docs/claude/gotchas.md, Gotcha 10).
   ============================================================================ */
---

<div class="scroll-progress" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<style>
  .scroll-progress {
    display: none;
  }

  @supports (animation-timeline: scroll()) {
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      .scroll-progress {
        display: block;
        position: fixed;
        inset-inline: 0;
        top: 0;
        z-index: 50;
        height: 3px;
        pointer-events: none;
        /* A flat accent fill (gradients and glows were retired in 2.1). */
        background-color: var(--color-accent);
        transform-origin: 0 50%;
        /* scaleX is cheap: a width change would relayout every frame. */
        transform: scaleX(0);
        animation: ncs-scroll-progress linear both;
        animation-timeline: scroll(root block);
      }
    }
  }

  @keyframes ncs-scroll-progress {
    to {
      transform: scaleX(1);
    }
  }

  @media print {
    .scroll-progress {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
